## Feature Request

## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.
I'm currently using Nestjs with firebase functions. As I need to load several modules due to coldstart, the startup time is very high, some cases almost 3 seconds

## Describe the solution you'd like
it would be interesting to have some way to load the modules as they are being requested. For example, I have the "orders" module that uses the "products" module and I also have the "users" module.
When I make a request to get user information, the "products" and "orders" modules are loaded, but will not be used (now).
In this case, these modules consume a lot of time in the coldstart even if they have not been used yet. This module may be used later or not, as this is the context of serverless functions


## Teachability, Documentation, Adoption, Migration Strategy

I thought of something like that:
The default behavior is what happens today, this "asLazy" option would be to enable lazy loading
```ts

@Module({
	controllers: [UsersController],
	providers: [UsersService],
	exports: [UsersService],
})
export class UsersModule {}

@Module({
	imports: [],
	controllers: [ProductsController],
	providers: [ProductsService],
	exports: [ProductsService],
})
export class ProductsModule {}

@Module({
	imports: [
		Example01Module, // these module will be loaded after the "OrdersModule" is loaded
		ProductsModule.asLazy(), // these modules will only be loaded after being requested inside "OrdersModule"
		UsersModule.asLazy(),
	],
	controllers: [OrdersController],
	providers: [OrdersService],
	exports: [OrdersService],
})
export class OrdersModule {}
@Module({
	imports: [
		Example01Module, // this module loads when the application starts (default behavior)
		Example02Module, // this module loads when the application starts (default behavior)
		Example03Module, // this module loads when the application starts (default behavior)
		ProductsModule.asLazy(), // these modules will only be loaded after being requested
		OrdersModule.asLazy(), // these modules will only be loaded after being requested
		UsersModule.asLazy(), // these modules will only be loaded after being requested
	],
	controllers: [OrdersController],
	providers: [OrdersService],
	exports: [OrdersService],
})
export class AppModule {}


```
## What is the motivation / use case for changing the behavior?
Serverless. Nest initialization is very slow when trying to apply to the serverless context.
A very lean application loads with 2 or 3 seconds (in firebase functions). If you add that to the computation time, the response would be very high. This reduction in coldstart time would reduce costs ($$) and create a better user experience.

There was an issue in 2019 with the same request, but it didn't go ahead. I believe there was a lack of context when creating it. (https://github.com/nestjs/nest/issues/2587)

## Feature Request

## Is your feature request related to a problem? Please describe.
I believe that the way Nestjs is bootstraped could be improved by allowing the definition of the container before the definition of the application.

Take as an example a `ConfigService` that would read all the variables in `.env` in order to use them as options to define a new Nestjs Microservice while having the following code, how would you do it?
```
async function bootstrap() {
    const app = await NestFactory.createMicroservice(AppModule, {
        transport: Transport.RMQ,
        options: {
            urls: [
                'amqp://guest:guest@localhost:5672',
            ],
            queue: 'test',
            queueOptions: { durable: true },
        },
    });
    ...
}
```
At the moment only 2 solutions come in mind:
- define an `ApplicationContext` to be able to get the `ConfigService` from the container to use it for instantiating the microservice.
- define a service that should be instantiated manually before without `DependencyInjection`

## Describe the solution you'd like
I believe that this could be done by instantiating the `ApplicationContext` that loads up the `Container` and then use the `ApplicationContext` as a parameter for the `NestFactory.createMicroservice` method.

## Teachability, Documentation, Adoption, Migration Strategy
In order to support backwards compatibility you could even allow both the current way in case the paramenter a Module or the following one in case it is an instance of an `ApplicationContext`
```
async function bootstrap() {
    const app = await NestFactory.createApplicationContext(AppModule);

    const config = app.get(ConfigService);

    const microservice = await NestFactory.createMicroservice(app, {
        transport: Transport.RMQ,
        options: {
            urls: [
                config.amqpUrl,
            ],
            queue: config.queue,
            queueOptions: { durable: true },
        },
    });
    ...
}
```
## What is the motivation / use case for changing the behavior?
- I believe this could improve the quality of the code. 
- Could be useful in order to add a single instance of a `LoggerService` via `DependecyInjection` and also allow setting `config` options on it in order to call external services like `Logstash` etc.

